WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.525
Same blasts, different meaning.

2
00:00:02.775 --> 00:00:09.950
This slide is the single biggest Inland difference on the exam, so let’s take the table one row at a time.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.075
One short blast.

4
00:00:12.325 --> 00:00:15.875
International: I am altering course to starboard.

5
00:00:16.125 --> 00:00:19.350
Inland: I intend to leave you on my port side.

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.500
Two short blasts.

7
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:25.050
International: I am altering course to port.

8
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.900
Inland: I intend to leave you on my starboard side.

9
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:34.350
Three short blasts mean the same in both: I am operating astern propulsion.

10
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.325
When are they used?

11
00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:40.825
International, whenever you maneuver in sight of another vessel.

12
00:00:41.075 --> 00:00:46.850
Inland, when power-driven vessels are meeting or crossing within half a mile of each other.

13
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.675
The reply.

14
00:00:48.925 --> 00:00:51.625
International, none is required.

15
00:00:51.875 --> 00:00:54.475
Inland, the other vessel answers.

16
00:00:54.725 --> 00:01:00.150
If she agrees, she sounds the same signal back, and both of you take steps to pass.

17
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.975
If she doubts it’s safe, she sounds the danger signal.

18
00:01:04.225 --> 00:01:05.725
And the light.

19
00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:08.125
International, it’s white.

20
00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:13.125
Inland, it may be white or yellow, and it’s flashed in time with the whistle.

21
00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:16.125
So remember it like this.

22
00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:18.325
Offshore, I’m telling you.

23
00:01:18.575 --> 00:01:20.575
Inshore, I’m asking you.

24
00:01:20.825 --> 00:01:23.500
International signals are statements.

25
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.375
Inland signals are proposals that need an answer.
